THOUGHT: NullAway now complains that iterator2 is @Nullable when dereferenced; I need to keep the lazy dual-iterator logic but ensure NullAway sees iterator2 as non-null on those paths. I can do this by adding local variables assigned from iterator1/iterator2 after the null check/initialization, and then using those locals instead of the fields directly. This preserves behavior and satisfies the analysis. I’ll patch the iterator() method accordingly and re-run the build to confirm the original METHOD_NO_INIT error is gone and no new errors are introduced.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '375,391s/^	  public IntSetIterator iterator() {$/	  public IntSetIterator iterator() {
	    if (Collections.allocateIterators) return new IntSetIterator(this);
	    if (iterator1 == null) {
	      iterator1 = new IntSetIterator(this);
	      iterator2 = new IntSetIterator(this);
	    }
	    IntSetIterator it1 = iterator1;
	    IntSetIterator it2 = iterator2;
	    if (!it1.valid) {
	      it1.reset();
	      it1.valid = true;
	      it2.valid = false;
	      return it1;
	    }
	    it2.reset();
	    it2.valid = true;
	    it1.valid = false;
	    return it2;
	  }/' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/IntSet.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -n "IntSet.java" new_build_output.log || true && grep -n "NullAway" new_build_output.log | tail -n 10 || true && grep -n "errors" new_build_output.log | tail -n 5 || true
```

